window.STORIES[1] = "Luna got into her bed again.\n\nThis was not a surprise. Luna had done this enough times that Aoi had stopped asking why, partly because the reasons were always different and equally implausible, and partly because she'd realised at some point that asking gave Luna an audience, and Luna with an audience was worse than Luna just being silently in her bed. So: here they were. Aoi on her back, Luna somewhere slightly to her left and probably sprawled, because Luna slept like a person who had never considered that other people existed in space.\n\nAoi stared at the ceiling.\n\nShe wasn't going to sleep. She could feel that in the way you can feel it \u2014 the particular flatness behind the eyes that's too tired to be alert and too wired to drop off. She'd felt it all day, this weight that wasn't heavy so much as just... constant. Like static. The kind of tired that work makes you, not the kind that sleep fixes.\n\nThe kind, she did not let herself think further about.\n\nLuna shifted and ended up closer. Aoi didn't move away.\n\nThis was new, technically. She would normally have moved. She would normally have said something \u2014 sleep on your own side, I'm serious, you're like a furnace \u2014 and there would have been the back-and-forth of it, Luna being insufferable, Aoi drawing a line, and then some version of the same standoff they'd been having since October. She didn't do any of that. She just lay there with Luna's warmth at her side and kept staring at the ceiling, and the ceiling had nothing to offer.\n\n---\n\nThe thing about her family was that people didn't get it unless they'd lived it, and she'd long since stopped explaining. The shorthand version \u2014 parents, brother, the usual story \u2014 didn't carry any of the actual texture of it, the way it felt to grow up understanding you were the wrong shape for the space you'd been given. Her brother had fit that space perfectly and had never, as far as she could tell, thought about it for a single day in his life. Her parents had softened eventually, as people do when time does the work that communication couldn't, but by then she'd already put the distance there herself and found she didn't know how to close it. Spite had calcified into habit. Habit had calcified into just \u2014 how things were.\n\nShe wasn't thinking about that specifically. She was not, in fact, thinking about much. That was the thing about this kind of tired, the variant that work had been giving her lately; it emptied you out and then left you there, hollow, unable even to properly ruminate. She would have preferred to ruminate. Rumination at least had momentum.\n\nLuna breathed next to her. Slow. Already under.\n\nAoi found, without quite meaning to, that she'd turned slightly, that one arm had ended up loosely around Luna, that her face was angled toward the window rather than the ceiling. She could see the edge of the city through the gap in the curtain, the orange-grey that passed for dark in a place where nothing was ever fully dark. Some building's light. Some street's particular glow.\n\nShe didn't know when she'd started holding on. She hadn't decided to.\n\n---\n\nLuna woke up, and Aoi was still there.\n\nThis was, in the particular logic of how things usually went between them, not the expected outcome. The expected outcome was Aoi coming to consciousness at some slightly unreasonable hour and immediately registering Luna as a problem to be solved. Complaint. Negotiation. Luna getting the shoulder. The routine of it.\n\nInstead: Aoi, awake, very still, arms around her, staring at the window. Not asleep. Not going back to sleep. Just \u2014 there, in the dark, with that look on her face that Luna didn't have a name for because she had never seen it on Aoi before.\n\nShe didn't move. Didn't say the usual thing. Aoi hadn't moved either \u2014 hadn't registered that Luna was awake, or had registered it and didn't react, which was somehow worse.\n\n\"Aoi.\"\n\nA beat.\n\n\"Hm?\"\n\nLike everything was fine. Like she'd just been asked if she'd eaten. That specific flatness in a voice that normally had bite to it, or impatience, or at minimum the particular energy of someone who found Luna's existence moderately exhausting. There was none of that. There was nothing.\n\nLuna kept very still.\n\nShe didn't know what to do here. That was the honest accounting of it. She and Aoi were not \u2014 they weren't that. Luna irritated Aoi, Aoi tolerated Luna, somewhere in there was an arrangement that had started as adversarial and had worn smooth at the edges without ever becoming something else, or nothing that had a name. This was not the arrangement calling for anything in particular from her. She was the one who got kicked out of the bed. She was not the one who knew what to do when the kicking stopped.\n\nSo she did nothing. Lay there. Listened to the city doing its 2 AM version of itself. Aoi didn't move. The window held whatever Aoi was looking for in it and didn't give it back.\n\n---\n\nThe sigh, when it came, was not a normal sigh. It was the kind that had been stored somewhere too long \u2014 shaky at the edges, the sound of a person trying to let out a small amount of pressure and discovering there was more behind it than they'd planned for.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Aoi said.\n\nLuna waited for the rest of it. There was no rest of it.\n\nShe wasn't apologising for anything that had happened tonight. That was clear enough. There was no inflection of specificity to it, no you-in-particular. It was the apology of someone who had gotten tired of carrying themselves around and had addressed it in the only direction available.\n\nLuna did not say it's okay. Did not say you don't need to apologise. She'd thought of both and they were wrong, both of them, the kind of words that exist to give the speaker somewhere to put the discomfort rather than because they're true. So she said nothing. Kept her hand where it was, loosely, not making anything of it. Just the fact of her being there, which she couldn't do much about at this point.\n\nThe silence settled back.\n\nThen: a tear. Not dramatic \u2014 Aoi wasn't doing anything dramatic, wasn't shaking or heaving. It was just a tear, and then after a while another one, and then a third, in the way water comes through a crack in something that's been holding for too long. Her face was still mostly in control. Her body was not consulting her face.\n\nLuna felt it before she saw it \u2014 the slight change in breathing, the way Aoi's grip shifted, tighter, without Aoi seeming to notice she'd done it. She didn't say anything. She pulled Aoi closer in a way that tried not to be a statement about itself, just a small adjustment, just arrangement of space. Started moving her hand in slow circles on Aoi's back. Not comfort exactly \u2014 she didn't have the language for that, never had. Just something to do with the hand. Just evidence.\n\nIt took a long time to become crying properly. The process was grinding and ugly, not the clean release of a good cry but the kind where the body is working against itself, where numbness fights the breaking down even as it's happening. Aoi went through it without speech, without any of the words people usually fill crying with. Luna rubbed her back and kept her mouth shut.\n\nThe wet came through the fabric. That was when she knew it had been going on for a while.\n\nShe thought about what she knew about Aoi's life, in the way you know things about a person you've been in proximity to \u2014 not because they told you, exactly, but because being in someone's space means you absorb it through the walls, through the phone calls you're not meant to hear, through the way she moved after a shift at work versus the way she moved any other time. The family situation. The brother. The work that was doing something to her, gradually, in the way certain jobs did \u2014 not a single wound but erosion. Nothing in her life sitting right, and instead of saying that to anyone she'd just kept shedding the people who might have noticed.\n\nLuna had stayed. Not for a reason she could make sound good. Aoi had said things, across the months, that landed worse than she'd probably meant them to, or maybe exactly as she'd meant them to, and Luna had stayed anyway, not as an act of virtue but just because \u2014 Aoi was there. Aoi was *there*, every day, difficult and tired and occasionally horrible and real in a way that made the apartment feel like a place someone actually lived rather than a space Luna was between things in. That was the whole of it. No poetry.\n\n---\n\n\"Why do you tolerate me.\"\n\nShe'd known it was coming. Or something like it. The question when you've been crying at someone for the better part of an hour and you can't explain why and you need some shape put to the incomprehensibility of another person choosing to stay in the room.\n\nLuna didn't answer immediately.\n\nThe answer, she knew, was not going to be satisfying. There was no version of it that would land as comfort, nothing she could say that would sound like enough reason. That was just the truth. You didn't have good reasons for the things that turned out to matter; you had the thing itself, the accumulated fact of it, and that was all.\n\n\"You were just \u2014 there,\" she said. \"When you didn't have to be either.\"\n\nAoi didn't say anything. Luna pulled the blanket over both of them \u2014 the room was cold, the heating in the building being the sort of suggestion rather than a system \u2014 and that was that. No resolution to it. Just the answer, sitting there, inadequate and honest.\n\n---\n\nAoi's breathing changed at some point, the ragged quality of it softening as the crying ran out of something to run on. Not peace \u2014 she wasn't peaceful, she wouldn't be peaceful for a while \u2014 but the specific exhaustion of a person who had used everything up and had nothing left to hold the rest of it with. She tucked herself in tighter, face under Luna's chin.\n\nLuna kissed the top of her head without thinking. It was out before she'd decided to do it, too small to call a decision. She left her mouth there a second and then didn't, just her chin against Aoi's hair, both of them still.\n\nAoi didn't say anything about it. Maybe she hadn't registered it. Maybe she had and it was the wrong moment to register it, or the right moment, or some other category that didn't have a name.\n\nShe went under slowly. Not a soft sleep but a falling one, the particular graceless drop of someone too tired to manage the transition. Luna held on. The city did its thing outside \u2014 quieter now, or it felt quieter, sound and space both settling into whatever 3 AM was. She stayed awake for a while after. Not for any reason. Just \u2014 awake.\n\n---\n\nThe sound that woke them later wasn't anything. A door, maybe. Nothing.\n\nThey were close. Faces close, in the way sleep arranges things without permission. Warm. The hour had gone soft around them, 2:50 something, and Luna was half-under still and Aoi was half-there, and the dark had that particular quality where the usual metrics didn't apply and things were mostly just proximity and breathing.\n\nThe kiss was not a decision that got made. It was the outcome of two people being too close together at too strange an hour, of something that had been in the room already, for longer than either of them had let themselves notice. There was the brushing of it, accidental or not-quite-accidental, and then there was the moment of staying, of not pulling back, of it becoming itself \u2014 brief and quiet and real, not tentative, not performed.\n\nThen it was over.\n\nNeither of them moved. The city. The dark. Whatever had just been confirmed between them sat in the room without commentary, because there was nothing to say at 3 AM that wouldn't make it smaller than it was.\n\nThey went back to sleep.\n\n---\n\nMorning was grey and thin and mostly uneventful.\n\nThey woke tangled \u2014 that was the word for it, no other word fit \u2014 legs overlapped, Aoi's hand somewhere near Luna's ribs, faces too close, the full inconvenient inventory of two people who'd slept in the same narrow bed without maintaining any of the usual spatial treaties. The light made everything specific. Aoi surfaced first and lay there for a moment registering all of it, the physical data of the night, and then sat up.\n\n\"Sorry,\" she said. Her voice was the morning version, flat and low. \"For being \u2014 sorry. All of it.\"\n\nLuna had her by the wrist before she could get any further.\n\nNot hard. Just \u2014 there. A fact.\n\nAoi looked at her hand and then at Luna, who was horizontal and sleep-creased and giving her the expression of someone who had already decided something and was not going to be argued with.\n\n\"Stay,\" Luna said.\n\nNot a question.\n\nAoi stayed.\n";
window.STORIES[2] = "The thing about Luna's job was that it required her to be a person all day.\n\nThis was not, technically, a complaint. She liked people, mostly \u2014 liked the specific texture of being around them, the way conversations had momentum of their own and took you places you hadn't planned to go. She was good at it, even. People responded to her in ways that surprised her sometimes, given that she wasn't trying to do anything in particular, just being whatever she was that day. But being whatever she was that day, reliably, eight-plus hours at a stretch, turned out to cost something. By the time she was on the train home she'd feel it \u2014 not in her legs or her back the way she might from physical work, but in a more general sense of having been running and now slowing, the particular winding-down of a machine that had been doing its job all day.\n\nThe train was loud in the way commuter trains always were \u2014 collective human noise, muffled conversations, the shudder and sway of it. She stood for most of the ride because it was that hour and there were no seats, one hand around the rail overhead, her bag hanging against her hip. Around her people were on their phones or staring at the middle distance with the particular expressionlessness of people who had decided not to be anywhere until they got somewhere.\n\nShe recognised it. She was probably doing it too.\n\n---\n\nThe walk from the station was ten minutes, which usually she liked \u2014 it was the decompression, the buffer between being out in it and being home. Tonight she just walked it. Noticed the usual things without really looking: the convenience store on the corner still bright, a dog being walked by someone in a coat already, the specific chill that had moved in since last week that meant seasons were shifting.\n\nThe apartment was on the third floor, no elevator. She took the stairs the way she always did, which was to say she didn't think about them.\n\nThe lights were on inside. Aoi was home.\n\nThis was not unusual \u2014 Aoi's hours were different, more structured, she was usually back by six. But Luna still registered it, the same way she registered it every time: the apartment's quality changed when someone else was in it. The difference between empty space and inhabited space, which is hard to articulate and completely obvious when you're in it.\n\nShe put her bag down and her shoes off and went to the kitchen to figure out if there was anything to eat that wasn't work.\n\n---\n\nShe made rice. This was the path of least resistance and also she was hungry in the specific way where complicated food sounded like too many decisions. While it cooked she leaned against the counter and looked at her phone for a while without reading anything on it, just scrolling the way you do when your eyes want somewhere to be.\n\nFrom the other room she could hear nothing. Not silence exactly \u2014 there was always background, the building, the street below, some neighbour's television at low volume \u2014 but nothing from Aoi specifically. No typing, no movement, no sounds of the usual end-of-day activity.\n\nShe thought about calling through to ask if she'd eaten. Didn't, for no particular reason.\n\nWhen the rice was done she took her bowl and went through to the main room.\n\n---\n\nAoi was at her desk. Not working, as far as Luna could tell \u2014 the laptop was open but its light was secondary to the window, which Aoi was looking through rather than at anything specific. The trees outside caught the orange of the residential lights on their undersides, dark above and lit below, and beyond them the sky had gone that particular evening navy that wasn't quite dark enough to be night but had given up being dusk. The street below was quieter than it would be in an hour, when people finished eating and walked their dogs and did the things people did at 8 PM. Right now there was just the occasional car, the occasional voice.\n\nAoi didn't turn around.\n\nLuna sat down on the floor by the couch, which was where she usually sat when she was eating in here, her back to the seat cushions. She ate a bit. The rice was fine. Outside, something flickered \u2014 a cyclist going past, the sweep of their light across the low branches.\n\nShe wasn't thinking about last night. She was aware of last night the way you're aware of a change in weather \u2014 you don't have to think about it for it to have changed how everything feels slightly different. It was there. Neither of them had said anything today, in the morning when they'd managed coffee and the slow process of being upright and human again. Not avoidance so much as \u2014 there not being anything yet that fit into words. That was okay. Luna was not in a hurry about it.\n\nShe watched Aoi not doing anything.\n\nThere was something she'd always noticed about Aoi when Aoi thought she wasn't being watched, which was that she became less defended. The deliberateness went out of her posture. She sat differently \u2014 not worse, just more like a person who had briefly stopped maintaining anything. It didn't happen often. Luna had catalogued the specific conditions under which it occurred, not intentionally, just because she paid attention to things.\n\nRight now was one of them. Aoi had her elbow on the desk and her chin on her hand, and she was looking at the trees and whatever was beyond the trees that couldn't be seen from here, and she looked tired in a way that had nothing to do with sleep.\n\nLuna finished her rice.\n\n---\n\nShe put the bowl on the floor beside her and stayed where she was, back against the couch. The room was dim \u2014 Aoi hadn't turned on the overhead, just the desk lamp, which didn't do much and seemed like a concession to the idea of working rather than actual working. It made the room feel smaller and warmer than it was, and the window more like a painting of outside than outside itself.\n\nShe should do things. There were things. Laundry that had been suggestions for three days. Something she'd meant to look up. A message she'd been composing in her head during the commute.\n\nShe stayed where she was.\n\nThere was a specific kind of quiet that happened when two people were in a room and neither of them needed anything from the other. Luna had lived with enough people \u2014 family, flatmates, various transient arrangements \u2014 to know it was rarer than it should be. Most shared quiet had something underneath it, some negotiation in progress, some awareness of the other person that required management. This was different. Aoi wasn't performing not-talking. Luna wasn't performing comfortable with the silence. They were just both in the room and both tired and both looking at the same window from different angles.\n\nLuna thought, not for the first time, that she liked this apartment best at this hour. Not because of anything in particular. Just the hour itself, the way the city got quieter without going away, the way the light outside changed from a daytime kind to an evening kind that was less demanding.\n\n---\n\nShe thought about Aoi's family the way she sometimes found herself thinking about things she'd absorbed without meaning to \u2014 the phone calls she'd half-heard through walls, the particular flatness Aoi got when she'd just gotten off one. She didn't have the full shape of it and she'd never asked, because Aoi was not someone you asked directly unless you wanted the shutters to come down immediately, and also because the details didn't feel like the point. The point was just: Aoi had been carrying something for a long time and had gotten very good at carrying it alone, and was not particularly skilled at knowing what to do when she didn't have to do that.\n\nLuna was not sure what she was, in Aoi's life, that had made last night possible. The proximity of it, probably \u2014 just that she'd been there, consistently, in the aggravating way she'd been there, until she wasn't a stranger anymore even if she wasn't anything nameable either. She hadn't done anything in particular to earn it. She'd just stayed in the general area long enough.\n\nThis was either a very simple thing or a very complicated one. She hadn't decided which.\n\n---\n\nAt some point Aoi turned from the window.\n\nNot toward Luna specifically \u2014 she just turned, the way you do when you've been looking at one thing for too long, redirecting without destination. She looked at the room, at the bowl on the floor, at Luna, with the slight adjustment of someone remembering she'd been in here.\n\n\"Did you eat?\" she said.\n\n\"Yeah.\" Luna indicated the bowl. \"Made rice.\"\n\nAoi looked at the bowl and then back at the window, or past it. \"Okay.\"\n\nShe didn't say anything else. She didn't turn back to the laptop either. Just sat there in the medium dark with her hand still on her chin.\n\nLuna looked at her for a moment, then looked at the window she'd been looking at. The trees were doing what trees did at 7 PM in autumn, which was to say they were just there, losing their outlines to the dark but still present. You could still tell they were trees. The light on their undersides from the streetlamps was orange and specific, and above them the sky was the colour of something not quite started.\n\n\"It's nice,\" Luna said, about nothing in particular.\n\nA pause.\n\n\"Yeah,\" Aoi said.\n\nAnd that was it. That was the whole of it. Luna stretched her legs out in front of her, crossed them at the ankle, and settled against the couch, and Aoi stayed where she was, and they both looked at the window and whatever was beyond the window, and the city did its evening things around them quietly and without requiring anything in response.\n\nLuna felt the specific weight of tired that her body had been accumulating all day settle into the floor through her, and thought, briefly and without particular drama, that this was fine.\n\nThis was enough.\n";
window.STORIES[3] = "The evening happened, as it did, weirdly calm in its comfortable silence. Luna had been rummaging in the kitchen, looking for whatever to eat, maybe a cold beer for the night - It'd be nice to just have a cold can, enjoy the silence, the day following was a weekend, and she'd wanted to relax. Aoi watched idly, just as numb as the evening sky.\n\nAoi stared at Luna, she was rummaging like a famished teen searching for snacks again. \"What are you even looking for...?\", she'd asked, almost tiredly, just as much mentally checked out.\n\nLuna, just looked at Aoi, peeking from the kitchen rack for just a moment, \"Hungry... bored.\".\n\"Oh... 'kay...\"\n\"You want somethin' too?\"\n\"I don't even think we have anything worthwhile, though...\"\n\"Eh... Chotto\u2026 Meiwaku.\", Luna just made this face, she knew they didn't stock up on the finest snacks... Didn't need to be reminded though.\n\"Don't make that face at me.\"\n\"I'm not making a face.\"\n\"You were about to.\"\n\"I was going to ask if there's anything else then, you're judging me too much. Hmph.\"\n\"There's rice.\"\n\nA beat.\n\nLuna stared blankly. Rice, really? Was dinner just supper now? At least they had furikake, a beer or two in the fridge...\n\n---\n\nSo they ate. Simple enough - whatever they could cobble together, tofu, rice, the furikake Luna had grabbed like it was a personal victory. Two cold cans between them on the small table, takeout containers nudged to one side to make room. Luna cracked hers open first. Aoi followed, a moment later, like she'd been waiting to see if Luna would first.\n\nThe overhead light was too bright. It always was. Neither of them had done anything about it.\n\n\"How was today...,\" Luna asked, not really asking.\n\n\"Fine.\"\n\nLuna gave her a look.\n\n\"That's not an answer, y'know.\"\n\n\"It's a short one.\"\n\n\"...Mm.\" She drank. \"Mine was stupid. Had a meeting. Then another one about the first one.\"\n\nAoi just looked at her. \"That's most of work.\"\n\n\"Is yours like that?\"\n\n\"Mine's just\u2014\" Aoi paused, like she was trying to find the word for it. \"A lot. All at once. Then nothing for twenty minutes. Then a lot again.\"\n\n\"That sounds worse than meetings, honestly.\"\n\nAoi shrugged, the smallest one. \"It is what it is.\"\n\n---\n\nThey ate for a while. The pot on the stove had gone cold, clicking quietly to itself. Outside, something \u2014 a car, a bike, nothing \u2014 passed and faded. Luna was looking at the window. Their reflections in it, slightly ghost-like, the smeared orange of the streetlamps beyond the glass.\n\n\"Do you ever just... not want to go back,\" she said.\n\n\"To work?\"\n\n\"In the morning. When the alarm goes off.\"\n\nAoi's chopsticks stopped.\n\n\"Everyone does.\"\n\n\"I know. I'm asking if you do.\"\n\nA beat.\n\n\"...Yeah.\"\n\n\"What do you do about it.\"\n\n\"Go anyway.\"\n\nLuna nodded slowly. Just, yeah. Of course. What else was there.\n\n\"Yeah...\"\n\n---\n\nThey finished eating. Or mostly \u2014 Aoi had pushed her rice around for a bit before setting her chopsticks down like that was a decision. Luna was on her second half of the can, not rushing it, just letting the cold of it sit in her hand.\n\n\"Can I ask you something,\" she said.\n\n\"You're going to regardless.\"\n\n\"Your family.\"\n\nAoi put her chopsticks down properly this time.\n\n\"...You don't have to,\" Luna added. \"I've just heard the calls. I don't really know what I'm hearing.\"\n\nA long pause. Aoi picked up her beer. Drank.\n\n\"It's not interesting.\"\n\n\"I'm not asking 'cause it's interesting.\"\n\nAoi looked at her then, just for a second. Set the can down carefully.\n\n\"My brother's better at everything. Or he was, or\u2014\" she stopped. \"It doesn't matter. My parents wanted something I wasn't. I was angry about it for a while and then I got tired of being angry and then I just... stopped going home as much. And they let me.\"\n\n\"They let you.\"\n\n\"It was easier. For everyone.\"\n\n\"...Including you?\"\n\nA beat.\n\n\"No.\"\n\nJust the fact of it, flat, no performance attached. Luna turned her beer in her hands, not drinking, just holding the cold of it.\n\n\"My mum used to do this thing...,\" she started, \"\u2014 every time I called she'd ask if I was happy like it was a maths problem. Like there was a right answer and I was just taking too long with it.\"\n\nAoi blinked. \"Were you? Taking too long.\"\n\nSomething flickered in Luna's expression - she made this face, somewhere between caught and amused. \"...Probably.\"\n\nAnd Aoi's mouth did something. Not quite a smile. Close, though. Closer than usual.\n\n---\n\nThe table between them was small. Had always been small. Right now it felt more like a feature than a flaw.\n\n\"Are you,\" Aoi said, after a while.\n\n\"...What.\"\n\n\"Happy.\"\n\nLuna looked at her. Aoi was looking at her beer.\n\n\"Right now?\"\n\n\"In general.\"\n\nLuna thought about it - actually thought about it, which she didn't always do when people asked. \"I don't know... Some days. You?\"\n\nAoi traced the rim of the can with her thumb. A long beat. \"I don't think about it that much.\"\n\n\"Because it's easier not to.\"\n\n\"...Yeah.\"\n\nLuna got up then, took both cans - hers nearly empty, Aoi's still more than half - went to the fridge, got two more. Set one in front of Aoi without asking. Sat back down.\n\nAoi looked at the new can. Didn't say anything.\n\nDidn't need to.\n\n---\n\n\"You know,\" Luna said, at some point, her chin in her hand, \"the other night.\"\n\nAoi's hand stilled on the can.\n\n\"I'm not\u2014 I'm not doing anything about it,\" Luna added, not looking away. \"I just wanted to say. I'm glad you didn't kick me out.\"\n\n\"I kick you out all the time.\"\n\n\"You try. It doesn't stick.\"\n\nAoi made a face. The exasperated one, the one Luna knew well, except something underneath it was different tonight - softer, a little. \"Which part,\" she said carefully.\n\n\"Both parts.\"\n\nAoi looked at the window then. Their reflections again, the table between them, the two cans, the ordinary wreckage of a nothing dinner that had somehow turned into something else.\n\nLuna watched the side of her face.\n\n\"...It's fine,\" Aoi said, eventually. Quiet.\n\n\"Which part.\"\n\nA beat.\n\n\"This.\"\n\n---\n\nLong quiet after that. The earned kind - the kind you had to sit in for a while before you realised you were in it. Luna leaned back in her chair, looked at the ceiling. The overhead light buzzed just faintly. Outside the window the city was doing its late-evening thing, winding down without going away.\n\n\"We should get a better light in here,\" Luna said, to no one particularly.\n\n\"We should.\"\n\n\"I'll forget.\"\n\n\"Me too, probably.\"\n\nLuna smiled, a small one, at the ceiling. Aoi didn't see it.\n\nOr maybe she did, and chose not to comment. Hard to say, with her.\n\n---\n\nThey cleaned up in the way they'd gotten used to without deciding to - one rinsed, the other stacked, the leftovers went in the only container that still had a matching lid. No discussion about who did what. The kitchen light off. The hallway dim.\n\nAoi paused at her bedroom door, hand on the frame.\n\n\"...You can stay again,\" she said. Not looking back. \"If you're cold.\"\n\nLuna just stood there for a second.\n\n\"It's always cold.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nA beat, and then Aoi went in, and after a moment Luna followed, and the light under the door stayed on for a while before it didn't.\n";